keel-web-19.0 (2) keel; urgency=low

  * The usage screen lists the site. Core's recipe ships a
    /etc/confconsole/services.txt of Webmin and SSH only, and the web
    layer kept it, so the console never showed the site. The overlay now
    ships one that puts "Web: http://<address>" first, IPv6 and IPv4:
    port 80 serves the site in a simple installation and redirects to
    HTTPS behind Anubis, so the one URL is right in every mode.
    conf.d/main fails the build when the usage screen does not list it.
  * keel-web 0.1.1: the Anubis front serves HTTPS with the machine's
    certificate and port 80 redirects to it, so browsers pass Anubis's
    challenge (its cookies are Secure); it needs keel-overlay-coraza
    0.1.2, which lets the challenge pass by IP and names the rule that
    blocked in its audit log, and anubis 1.27.0-0+keel3, which serves the
    challenge to clients that do not accept gzip (closes #2) and
    redirects a solved challenge only to the request's own origin.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 04:00:00 +0000

keel-web-19.0 (1) keel; urgency=low

  * First release of Keel Web (handbook decisions 0030 and 0041, step 7
    of its first implementation), a layer on Keel Core: plan/main
    installs keel-web, the appliance manifest, which brings the nginx,
    coraza and anubis overlays. A simple installation is plain Nginx
    serving Debian's default site, with Coraza's module not loaded and
    Anubis stopped; the cloud modes turn both on through keel spec apply,
    and the anubis state hook of keel-web puts the default site behind
    Anubis.
  * The overlay ships /var/www/html/index.html, the page of the default
    site: common's removelist deletes /var/www/html, Debian's page with
    it, so the default site answered 404 on the first build.
  * conf.d/main fails the build on an Anubis signing key, a record of
    the overlays' state hooks, Anubis enabled, Coraza linked into Nginx,
    the Anubis front linked, Debian's default site not enabled, or no
    page for it.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0000
